I don't think you appreciate just how huge the inherent regeneration can
get. First of all, for a character over XL 9 [1] a RoR only helps by
permitting a character who is heavily encumbered and moving to heal up
(otherwise, you will not heal if heavily encumbered unless you stand
still). A character over XL 9 heals 2 in every 3 turns a random number of
points between 1 and Con, capped to XL minus 9.

Hence a 27+ XL character with Con 18 will heal 6 1/3 points a turn on
average. However, being Very Fast is good for 1 2/3 moves per turn on
average; hence Stormy is good for 3 3/4 points per turn on average in the
best possible case - ie, you do nothing but attack (and never miss, but
high-level characters don't) beasties who are not immune to level drain.
As soon as you start doing things other than attacking - eg, moving through
a crowd in order to get somewhere, as one _does_ in the late game - Stormy
gives an even lesser amount compared to natural healing.

You were only XL 19, but with 18 Con; this still, however, is good for an
average 5 points a turn; hence you were healing more - substantially more
- through natural healing than through Stormy even when repeatedly
attacking non-immune monsters.

Also stringing out fights by doing less damage but also taking less damage
carries another penalty; it wastes moves. In the early game food is an
issue, as is generation of fresh monsters - albeit not very significantly
so. In the middle game there is little penalty for wasting time; however,
in the late game - with Rodney dead and summoning monsters not uncommon,
there is a very significant penalty for doing so. If I can choose between
finishing a fight with Firebrand in 3 turns or with Stormy in 5 turns,
with the same expectation as to my HP at the finish, I will prefer the
faster kill.

Stormy is, I agree, not as bad as its reputation; but I think its main
strength is seen in the early game.

